module gunbc.spark.host_commitment

import std.types { Bool, List, String, NonEmptyStr }
import std.algebra { FreeMonoid, Empty, Cons, FreeSemigroup }
import v2.std.optional { Present, Absent }
import std.decl_ref { declaration_ref_display_key }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity, host_identity_eq }
import gunbc.spark.cell_role {
  SparkCellRole,
  SparkCellRoleAssignment,
  SparkCellRoleAbsent,
  SparkCellRoleUnique,
  SparkCellRoleAmbiguous,
  spark_cell_role_in,
  spark_cell_role_assignments,
}
import gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed { FabricGroup, fabric_group_hosts }
import gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired { spark_pair_serving_groups }
import gunbc.spark.llama_cpp_rpc_observed { llama_cpp_rpc_undischarged_peer_reservation_hosts }
import gunbc.spark.serving_arm_placement { ServingArmHostClaim, spark_serving_arm_host_claims }
import gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement { dgx_spark_reserved_identities }

// WHAT EACH DGX SPARK UNIT IS CURRENTLY SPOKEN FOR BY, AS A SET OF IDENTITIES WITH THEIR CAUSES.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS A SAFETY REPAIR RATHER THAN A REFINEMENT.
// gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection CARRIES, PRESENT TENSE AND STILL TODAY, the fleet as
// `host_count: 8`, and screens a candidate with `hosts_consumed(c) <= fleet.host_count`. A scalar
// cannot tell eight free hosts from eight of which five are spoken for -- and five of these eight
// ARE spoken for: the four hosts of the one claimed serving group, and the host the GLM canary is
// placed on -- so the one screen whose whole job
// is to rule shapes OUT is generous in exactly the direction DESIGN section 5 forbids, and generous
// silently, because nothing in a count can carry the fact that it was wrong. The repair is not a
// check placed beside the count: it is to delete the count and derive the SET, so that a shape too
// wide for the hosts that actually exist has no admitting arm to reach.
//
// THIS MODULE IS THE SET. IT IS NOT YET THAT REPAIR, and the distinction is stated here rather than
// left for a reader to discover, because a note that describes an intended end state in the past
// tense is how a live fail-open comes to look closed. The serving-selection cutover remains a
// follow-up outside this change: the selection screen still carries the scalar fleet snapshot and
// still admits unplaceable shapes, while this change adds the shared authority and rewires its
// build and probe consumers. The honest reading of this file is "the authority the repair needs,
// with its selection consumer still to arrive".
//
// THE SET IS DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORED. Every host list this module could have written already exists
// somewhere that owns it, and restating one here would be the second authority DESIGN section 3
// forbids -- the copy that goes stale while the original moves. So the inputs are inhabited rather
// than transcribed: the unit population is gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement's reserved identities; the
// standing role commitments are gunbc.spark.cell_role's assignments; the hosts of the serving groups
// this repository actually claims are gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired's groups read through
// gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed's observed cage membership; and the held reservations are
// gunbc.spark.llama_cpp_rpc_observed's undischarged internal-participant hosts. This module owns one
// fact and one only: that those JOIN, and how.
//
// AND THE JOIN ALREADY EXISTED, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF WHY THIS FILE IS HERE.
// gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build had authored `spark_build_host_excluded` /
// `admissible_spark_build_hosts` -- the same subtraction over two of the same authorities, to answer
// where a container build may run. A second consumer arriving with the same question is precisely the
// moment the fold stops being one module's private helper. So the fold moved here at its root, the
// build lane consumes it, and neither answers for the other.
//
// WHAT THAT MOVE DID AND DID NOT CHANGE, stated exactly, because an earlier revision of this note
// overstated it. It said the two derivations "differ by two hosts", which is false: the private build
// fold excluded the claimed serving group and the held reservations and answered srv9-srv12, and this
// module answered the same four until the serving-arm claim below was added. What changed is COVERAGE
// OF COMMITMENT CAUSES, not the membership either produced on the day. The scalar this whole exercise
// is aimed at -- `host_count: 8` in serving selection -- differs from the derived set by four, not
// two, and it is a count rather than a derivation, so it was never the second fork; it is the
// consumer that has not cut over yet.
//
// THIS SET ANSWERS THE NEW-DEPLOYMENT QUESTION, AND ONLY THAT ONE. ADMISSIBILITY IS SUBJECT-RELATIVE
// AND THIS MODULE IS NOT YET.
//
// The set answers: which units may receive work that NOTHING has placed yet. It does not answer
// whether a host is available TO A PARTICULAR SUBJECT, and those differ exactly where it matters. The
// four hosts of FabricGroupA are unavailable to a new deployment -- and they are precisely where the
// V4 Flash deployment already runs. So a subject's own commitment is availability to that subject and
// unavailability to everyone else, and one global set cannot say both.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE IS A SCOPE RULE FOR CONSUMERS, and it decided how DSV41-9's second half consumes
// this module (operator-level ruling via the DSV41 manager, 2026-09-12). A consumer asking "where may
// I place something new" -- the container build, the image probe, the V4.1 serving domain -- takes the
// set as its DOMAIN. A consumer that already has a placement -- the GLM and V4 Flash candidate fields,
// whose shapes were authored over the physical unit population -- must NOT have its domain narrowed by
// this set, because that would assert the shape running today cannot exist, and would change three
// decision field identities at once (DESIGN section 3d: a different candidate field is a different
// decision). What every consumer does take is the FIT: a shape wider than the admissible set is
// refused BY NAME against these identities, which is the fail-open this module was written to close,
// and refusing a shape that exists carries more than never enumerating it.
//
// TRIGGER FOR THE REFINEMENT THAT WOULD DISSOLVE THE SPLIT: admissibility relative to a named subject
// -- a standing that answers "available to THIS subject", where a host committed to that same subject
// counts as available to it and committed to any other counts as unavailable. SUFFICIENT FOR: the
// existing subjects' domains can consume admissibility too, because the V4 Flash domain asking about
// its own hosts gets them back rather than losing them. Until that exists, a global set consumed as a
// domain by a subject that already holds hosts is a narrowing, not a wall, and this note is what
// stops it being done by accident.

// A COMMITMENT IS NOT A CAPABILITY. Nothing here says a host cannot serve or cannot build. It says
// some other authority has already placed work on it, so a lane that places NEW work must not treat
// it as free. The remedy is always the same and always elsewhere: release the reservation, withdraw
// the group, or change the role. Every one of those widens this set with no edit to this file, which
// is the property that makes the derivation worth having over a list.
//
// WHY A CELL ROLE COMMITS, INCLUDING A SERVING ROLE. The obvious reading of an exclusion list is
// "hosts that cannot serve", and under that reading a SparkServingCell excluding itself is absurd.
// That is the wrong reading -- the question is which hosts are UNCOMMITTED. gunbc.spark.cell_role
// gives the physical reason in its own note: two roles on one unified memory pool have no arbiter.
// Placing a freshly selected deployment beside an already-converged one is that same contention
// arriving through a different door, and the remedy cell_role states for the training case is the
// general one -- a deployment that wants an assigned cell must CHANGE THE ASSIGNMENT, not run
// beside it.
//
// WHY AN UNDECIDABLE ROLE ALSO COMMITS. `spark_cell_role_in` answers a third arm: two rows naming one
// host, which no row's closed sum can prevent because the ambiguity is in the ROSTER rather than in
// any row. A lookup that cannot say what a host is committed to has not said the host is free, and
// reading it as free is the absence-as-fact inference this fleet's authorities keep refusing.
//
// THE SERVING-GROUP ARM IS THE ACTIVE GROUPS, NOT EVERY CAGE, and that distinction is inherited
// rather than re-reasoned: a FabricGroup is a 400G cage of four hosts, so subtracting every group
// subtracts the whole fleet. A cage is where a group COULD serve; spark_pair_serving_groups is where
// one DOES. The build lane learned that from a red witness and the reasoning is its, not this file's.
//
// THE SERVING-ARM CLAIM IS THE CAUSE THIS CENSUS SHIPPED WITHOUT, AND IT WAS A FAIL-OPEN.
//
// The first cut read cell roles, claimed pair-serving groups and held llama.cpp reservations, and
// declared srv9 uncommitted. srv9 is where the GLM canary is serving. gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment
// says so directly -- the canary is serving on srv9 and nothing in this repository launched it through
// the pair route -- and derives that route's head from the arm rather than from the pair roster,
// precisely because the pair claim on group B was withdrawn while the canary was not. An observer
// reading only the pair roster therefore sees srv9 as free and is wrong, and this module was that
// observer while claiming to be the authority on what every unit is spoken for by.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE WAS NOT ACADEMIC, which is why the cause is added rather than the note softened.
// Both consumers of this set authorise effects on the host they are handed: a source build that
// occupies a machine for most of an hour, and an image probe that pulls a 9.6 GB image and starts a
// container. Either, pointed at srv9, would have landed on a machine answering live requests.
//
// THE CLAIM IS READ FROM BELOW, NOT FROM THE ARM. gunbc.spark.serving_arm_placement carries the
// placement fact on its own, because the canary's convergence root imports serving selection, and
// serving selection imports THIS module in the follow-up -- reaching up into the canary module would
// close that loop. The placement authority also carries why release is a receipt rather than an edit,
// which is the same lifecycle the reservation arm below already enforces.
//
// THE HELD RESERVATION IS A WALL THAT IS CURRENTLY QUIET, NOT A DEAD ONE.
// `llama_cpp_rpc_undischarged_peer_reservation_hosts()` is EMPTY on this corpus today: the one minted
// internal-participant reservation (srv8, the weights peer) was cleared by a release receipt on
// 2026-09-05, as was srv7's, and test.claim.spark.spark_llama_cpp_rpc_witness asserts exactly that.
// So this arm commits nobody at the moment -- srv7 and srv8 are held by the serving group instead --
// and that is the reservation lifecycle working rather than an arm with nothing behind it: a positive
// observation mints, only a positive receipt clears, and not looking again is not a release. Its
// evidence is therefore taken over FIXTURE rosters, which is the same argument `spark_cell_role_in`
// makes for existing beside `spark_cell_role_of`: a capability's evidence must not depend on which
// machines currently hold what.

// A COMMITMENT IS A CAUSE, NOT A BOOLEAN, AND A HOST MAY HOLD MORE THAN ONE.
//
// A `Bool` here would answer the screen and lose the report, and a single-cause sum would force a
// precedence between facts that are independently true -- a host can be a serving-group member AND
// hold a reservation, and dropping either makes a later release receipt look like it freed a host it
// did not free.
type SparkHostCommitment
  = CommittedToCellRole { role: SparkCellRole }
  | MemberOfClaimedServingGroup
  | OccupiedByServingArm { claim: ServingArmHostClaim }
  | HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation
  | CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment> }

// UNCOMMITTED AND COMMITTED ARE DECIDED BY THE SAME DESTRUCTURE THAT SUPPLIES THE PAYLOAD.
//
// `SparkHostCommitted { commitments: [] }` -- a host spoken for by nothing -- is the state that must
// not be writable, and the way it is made unwritable is std.algebra's free semigroup, exactly as
// std.goal_assessment mints a divergence: the cause list is MATCHED, and the committed arm holds a
// head it was handed. An `if length(causes) == 0` fork would leave the empty commitment one edit away
// from being written.
type SparkHostStanding
  = SparkHostUncommitted { host: HostIdentity }
  | SparkHostCommitted { host: HostIdentity, commitments: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment> }

fn spark_host_commitments_in(
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_serving_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  host: HostIdentity,
) -> FreeMonoid<SparkHostCommitment> {
  let reserved =
    if any(held_reservation_hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)) {
      Cons { head: HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation, tail: Empty {} }
    } else {
      Empty {}
    }
  let armed =
    fold(
      filter(arm_claims, c => host_identity_eq(a: c.host, b: host)),
      init: reserved,
      f: fn(acc, c) { Cons { head: OccupiedByServingArm { claim: c }, tail: acc } },
    )
  let serving =
    if any(claimed_serving_hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)) {
      Cons { head: MemberOfClaimedServingGroup, tail: armed }
    } else {
      armed
    }
  match spark_cell_role_in(assignments: assignments, host: host) {
    SparkCellRoleAbsent => serving
    SparkCellRoleUnique { role: r } =>
      Cons { head: CommittedToCellRole { role: r }, tail: serving }
    SparkCellRoleAmbiguous { assignments: rows } =>
      Cons { head: CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: rows }, tail: serving }
  }
}

// A STANDING HAS EXACTLY ONE CONSTRUCTION SITE, AND IT IS THE TRAVERSAL OF THE POPULATION ITSELF.
//
// THIS IS THE THIRD CUT AT ONE DEFECT AND THE PREVIOUS TWO EXPLAIN THE SHAPE. The original producer
// took a host and four commitment lists and answered a STANDING; handed a machine that is not a DGX
// Spark unit it found no cause and said `SparkHostUncommitted`, which reads as "the fleet looked and
// nothing has claimed it" when the fleet had not looked. The first repair added the population and an
// Absent arm but moved the destructure into a `spark_standing_from_causes(host, causes)` helper --
// the same unrestricted mint one level down, because naming a helper "the mint" does not give it
// module privacy: `.dag` has no visibility modifier, so an unchecked constructor is a public one. The
// second repair deleted that helper and inlined the destructure behind a per-host membership scan.
//
// THAT SECOND REPAIR WAS CORRECT AND ITS ANNOTATION WAS WRONG, which is the part worth keeping. The
// note here offered a choice between a repeated membership scan and the return of an unchecked mint,
// and called the resulting quadratic the necessary price of one construction authority. It is not.
// DESIGN section 6 says a proven cost-shape defect is ALWAYS fixed regardless of the realized n, and
// names "n is small here" as not a time-stable justification -- so an eight-row roster does not
// discharge it, and pricing a per-site exception is the move that rule exists to refuse. The
// arithmetic was real: with n hosts and every commitment list empty, the outer traversal asked the
// inner producer to rediscover each host in the same roster, so membership work grew as n(n+1)/2 with
// no causes to join at all.
//
// THE DISTINCTION THE FALSE CHOICE MISSED: A CONSTRUCTOR THAT TAKES A POPULATION AND CONSTRUCTS ONLY
// WHILE TRAVERSING THAT POPULATION IS ALREADY POPULATION-BOUNDED. There is no independent host input
// at the construction site to check, because the host being constructed for CAME FROM the traversal
// -- membership follows from where it came from rather than from a scan asking whether it is there.
// That is not the deleted function, which accepted an arbitrary host with no population in sight. So
// the fold below is the sole construction authority, the destructure sits directly inside its walk of
// `units`, and the per-host answer is a LOOKUP over the constructed result rather than a second mint.
// One authority, no rescan, no proof type, and nothing for a later edit to route around.
//
// `spark_host_commitments_in` stays public and stays unchecked ON PURPOSE: it answers which of the
// lists you handed it name this host -- a question about the lists, not a claim about the fleet --
// and it returns a cause monoid rather than a standing, so it is not another standing-producing
// boundary and keeping it public does not recreate the deleted mint.

fn spark_host_standings_in(
  units: List<HostIdentity>,
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_serving_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
) -> List<SparkHostStanding> {
  map(units, h => match spark_host_commitments_in(
    assignments: assignments,
    claimed_serving_hosts: claimed_serving_hosts,
    arm_claims: arm_claims,
    held_reservation_hosts: held_reservation_hosts,
    host: h,
  ) {
    Empty => SparkHostUncommitted { host: h }
    Cons { head: first, tail: rest } =>
      SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: FreeSemigroup { head: first, tail: rest } }
  })
}

// THE PER-HOST ANSWER IS A LOOKUP OVER THAT RESULT, NOT A SECOND MINT. It constructs nothing: it asks
// the constructed population whether it holds the host, so an outside-population host has no standing
// to find and the Absent arm is a consequence of the roster rather than a check standing beside it.
fn spark_host_standing_in(
  units: List<HostIdentity>,
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  claimed_serving_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  arm_claims: List<ServingArmHostClaim>,
  held_reservation_hosts: List<HostIdentity>,
  host: HostIdentity,
) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  spark_standing_of(
    standings: spark_host_standings_in(
      units: units,
      assignments: assignments,
      claimed_serving_hosts: claimed_serving_hosts,
      arm_claims: arm_claims,
      held_reservation_hosts: held_reservation_hosts,
    ),
    host: host,
  )
}

// THE SET, AS IDENTITIES, AND IT IS A PROJECTION OF THE STANDINGS RATHER THAN A SECOND DERIVATION.
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES HAD TWO ROOTS. `spark_host_standings` folded the commitment sources, and
// `spark_uncommitted_hosts` folded the same sources again to answer the narrower question -- so the
// module whose whole purpose is to stop two derivations of one fact from drifting contained two
// derivations of one fact. A fifth commitment source added to one and not the other would have been
// invisible in exactly the way the private build fold was. These take a standings list and read it;
// there is nowhere in them for an input to be forgotten, because they have no inputs to forget.
fn spark_uncommitted_hosts_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(standings, s => match s {
    SparkHostUncommitted { host: h } => [h]
    SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: _ } => []
  })
}

fn spark_committed_standings_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>) -> List<SparkHostStanding> {
  filter(standings, s => match s {
    SparkHostUncommitted { host: _ } => false
    SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: _ } => true
  })
}

// The lookup folds the matched rows into an optional exactly as gunbc.spark.cell_role's own lookup
// does, rather than reaching for a list head this substrate does not expose as a free function.
fn spark_standing_of(standings: List<SparkHostStanding>, host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  fold(
    filter(standings, s => match s {
      SparkHostUncommitted { host: h } => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)
      SparkHostCommitted { host: h, commitments: _ } => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host)
    }),
    init: none,
    f: fn(acc, s) { Present { value: s } },
  )
}

// THE PRODUCTION APPLICATION, AND IT IS THE ONLY PLACE THE COMMITMENT SOURCES ARE NAMED TOGETHER.
// Everything below is a projection of this one value.
fn spark_claimed_serving_group_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(spark_pair_serving_groups, g => fabric_group_hosts(g: g))
}

data spark_host_standings: List<SparkHostStanding> = spark_host_standings_in(
  units: dgx_spark_reserved_identities(),
  assignments: spark_cell_role_assignments,
  claimed_serving_hosts: spark_claimed_serving_group_hosts(),
  arm_claims: spark_serving_arm_host_claims,
  held_reservation_hosts: llama_cpp_rpc_undischarged_peer_reservation_hosts(),
)

data spark_uncommitted_hosts: List<HostIdentity> = spark_uncommitted_hosts_of(standings: spark_host_standings)

fn spark_host_is_uncommitted(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  any(spark_uncommitted_hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host))
}

fn spark_host_standing(host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostStanding? {
  spark_standing_of(standings: spark_host_standings, host: host)
}

// WHY A HOST IS NOT AVAILABLE, RENDERED FROM THE STANDING ITSELF.
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES WAS A HAND-MAINTAINED SENTENCE. `spark_uncommitted_hosts_how` listed the
// producers in prose and claimed it could not go stale because it named them rather than transcribing
// their answer. That was half true and the wrong half was load-bearing: it was still a second list of
// the commitment sources, maintained by hand, and adding the serving-arm cause did not update it --
// measured, because adding that cause is exactly what happened. A refusal now renders the CAUSES THIS
// HOST ACTUALLY CARRIES, which no edit can desynchronise from the fold, and which tells an operator
// the one thing the generic sentence never did: which authority to go and talk to.
// ONE PASS AND ONE JOIN, BECAUSE THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES RE-COPIED ITS OWN SUFFIX.
//
// `spark_commitment_tail_wire` recursed down the tail and joined the head's rendering to the ENTIRE
// rendered remainder at every step, so each level copied the whole suffix again -- quadratic in the
// number of causes. DESIGN section 6 rules that a proven cost-shape defect is always fixed regardless
// of the realized n, and it is right to insist even where a host carries few causes: n is not a
// time-stable fact, and pricing a per-site exception is itself redundant work.
//
// THE OPERATIONS ARE INHABITED, AND THE SYMBOLS THAT SAY SO ARE NAMED HERE RATHER THAN DESCRIBED.
// `std.algebra` `algebra_carriers` carries an `AlgebraCarrier` row whose name is "FreeMonoid" with
// profile `FreeMonoidCollectionProfile`, and `std.algebra` `free_monoid_collection_templates`
// declares `map` and `join` among that profile's operations. So no local rendering fold is authored:
// the causes are mapped once and joined once through operations the carrier already has.
//
// THAT SENTENCE USED TO SAY "FreeMonoid inhabits the collection profile", AND THE REWRITE IS THE
// LESSON. A reviewer checking the claim grepped for the phrase "collection profile" -- taken from
// this annotation, because prose was all it had to grep -- found only this annotation, and reported
// that the method surface resolves nowhere in the tree (review 64412). The claim was true and my
// citation made it unverifiable, which to an honest audit is indistinguishable from false. That is
// DESIGN section 3's cite-the-symbol rule biting from the direction it is usually not felt: the cost
// of a bad citation is not only that it rots, it is that it makes a correct claim fail a real check.
//
// AND THE ANNOTATION IS NOT THE EVIDENCE, which section 4c settles: no Accepted program can read one.
// The executing evidence is
// `test.claim.spark.spark_host_commitment_witness` `a_multi_cause_host_renders_every_cause_with_its_separator`,
// which renders a host committed by a cell role AND a claimed serving group AND a held reservation at
// once and asserts all three causes and the separator survive. It exists because the finding above
// was right about a real gap even while wrong about the mechanism: every other test here exercises a
// SINGLE cause, and a passing single-element render is consistent with the separator logic being
// entirely broken.
fn spark_commitment_causes_text(c: FreeSemigroup<SparkHostCommitment>) -> String {
  let every_cause = Cons { head: c.head, tail: c.tail }
  every_cause.map(x => spark_host_commitment_wire(c: x) as String).join("; ")
}

fn spark_host_commitment_causes_wire(host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match spark_host_standing(host: host) {
    Absent => "that host is not a DGX Spark unit in gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement, so this fleet has no standing for it" as NonEmptyStr
    Present { value: standing } =>
      match standing {
        SparkHostUncommitted { host: _ } => "nothing has claimed that host" as NonEmptyStr
        SparkHostCommitted { host: _, commitments: c } => spark_commitment_causes_text(c: c) as NonEmptyStr
      }
  }
}

fn spark_host_commitment_wire(c: SparkHostCommitment) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    CommittedToCellRole { role: _ } => "committed to a Spark cell role" as NonEmptyStr
    MemberOfClaimedServingGroup => "a member of a serving group this repository claims" as NonEmptyStr
    OccupiedByServingArm { claim: c2 } => join(["a serving arm is placed on it (", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: c2.arm), ")"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    HoldsUndischargedRpcReservation => "holding an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation" as NonEmptyStr
    CellRoleUndecidable { assignments: _ } => "carrying more than one cell role row, so its commitment cannot be decided" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE ONE ADMISSION SEAM, BECAUSE THREE EFFECTFUL ROOTS WERE EACH ASKING IN THEIR OWN WORDS.
//
// THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES: gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build owned a typed `admit_spark_build_host`
// that exactly one of its two entries called; the other entry, and the published-image probe in
// gunbc.spark.v41_runtime_image_probe, each re-wrote the guard as a bare `if !uncommitted` with their
// own refusal sentence. Three roots, one of them typed, and the witness exercised only the typed one
// -- so a regression in either open-coded guard left the suite green while an effect ran on a claimed
// host. The seam belongs to the authority that owns the commitment fact rather than to whichever lane
// needed it first, so it lives here and all three roots consume it.
//
// IT IS AN ELIGIBILITY ANSWER AND IT IS NOT A LEASE. THIS IS A STATED DIVERGENCE (DESIGN section 3b),
// not an oversight, and the honest form of it is the whole point. `SparkHostUncommitted` is an
// OBSERVATION over modelled persistent claims: it says nobody has written down a claim on this host.
// It is not a lease, not a compare-and-set, and it adds no claim of its own, so two runs can both
// read the same host as uncommitted and both proceed. The generated fleet workflow does not close
// that gap either -- its concurrency key is `gunbc-host-mutation-${host}` over the EXECUTOR runner
// (srv1-srv4), while the Spark being mutated is a separate target input, so two runs dispatched
// through different executors can target one Spark without ever sharing a key.
//
// THE THREE-STEP SHAPE THIS OWES, stated so the gap is legible rather than papered over:
//   uncommitted-set observation -> candidate ELIGIBILITY only
//   target-host claim acquired  -> PERMISSION to mutate
//   terminal release receipt    -> host becomes claimable again
// This module answers the first and nothing else. TRIGGER for the second: the fabric/capacity lease
// authority (product.capacity.lease LeaseGrant, gunbc.fabric_event_log fabric_seat_acquire) gaining a
// subject shaped like a whole Spark host rather than a serving seat, at which point admission becomes
// acquire-and-hold and this function's refusal arm becomes the lease's. SUFFICIENT FOR: two
// concurrent runs against one target, dispatched through different executors, cannot both be
// admitted. Until then an admitted answer means "nothing has claimed it as of this read", and the
// name says so rather than implying a hold.
type SparkHostAdmission
  = SparkHostAdmitted { host: HostIdentity }
  | SparkHostRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn admit_uncommitted_host(raw: String, purpose: NonEmptyStr) -> SparkHostAdmission {
  let host = raw as HostIdentity
  if raw == "" {
    SparkHostRefused { cause: join(["no host was named for ", purpose as String, "; this entry takes one and does not pick"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if !spark_host_is_uncommitted(host: host) {
    SparkHostRefused {
      cause: join([
        raw, " is not admissible for ", purpose as String, ", because ",
        spark_host_commitment_causes_wire(host: host) as String,
        ". Uncommitted as of this read: ",
        join(map(spark_uncommitted_hosts, h => h as String), ", "),
        ". This is an eligibility read and not a held claim",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    SparkHostAdmitted { host: host }
  }
}

